## 3. Test Plan

Use the existing `codex-continuation-admission-test.js`, MCP `companion-continuation.test.js`, UI `companion-continuation.test.tsx` and built-browser `sdk-trace.spec.mjs` suites. Reuse their existing race and recovery cases. Add checks for the fixture and profile boundary, bounded response, generation transitions and corresponding operator states. Run the affected suites, Control UI typecheck and package integrity checks on the candidate. Capture their results separately from native observations. A fresh authenticated isolated Desktop session and manual access to the panel and chat are execution prerequisites; record their actual availability for each test. No restart of the ordinary desktop profile is part of this procedure.

**Native setup and timing.** Record the Desktop version, package and UI resource identities, both servers' observed roots and generation, and the fixture's current operation. Open two chats on the same disposable project. Assign a fresh fixture to each race or lifecycle case. Keep a second ready fixture for the independence check. In the fixture-only harness, allow the operator to pause the original receiving claim after publication or defer the bounded reply until the competing request has been observed. The checkpoint must leave the shared admission lock free, allow ordinary status reads and competing requests, and affect only the designated fixture. Release by an explicit test-operator action. A missing or timed-out checkpoint marks the observation incomplete. Never use elapsed time as proof of a non-start. Keep one single-click run entirely free of timing controls.

**Observed quantities.** Record actual gestures, Companion messages sent, work grants and corresponding real receiving turns as separate counts. In a chat-versus-Companion race a manually entered chat prompt already creates a chat turn. Record that prompt separately and identify which turn actually received the single work grant. If chat wins, expect zero Companion sends. If the Companion wins, expect one. Capture the losing route's own outcome. A silent view or an absent trace is an unknown count.

| criterion_id | scenario_id | test_route | environment | prerequisites_permissions | owner | expected_evidence | unavailable_action |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| AC-001 | SCN-001 | Provision an eligible fixture, then run setup and selection checks with foreign roots, ordinary Runs and copied records. | Disposable profile setup and Core suite | Isolated workspace and local setup access | Codex integration | Positive identity plus each rejected combination | Withhold designation if roots or disposable provenance cannot be established. |
| AC-001 | SCN-002 | Exercise both installed MCP entry points on valid and invalid bindings. In deterministic tests change the revision or deactivate between reserve, publish and claim. | MCP fixture suite and isolated candidate | Same candidate on both routes and fresh fixture per mutation case | Core maintainer | Decisions per route and zero grants for every denial | Record the unverified route or transition and withhold the scope result. |
| AC-001 | SCN-003 | Render ready and ineligible cases at 360 pixels and wide width. Navigate by keyboard, poll and inspect the same labels in the native panel. | Component suite, built browser and actual Desktop panel | Current UI bundle and selectable fixtures | Companion UI and maintainer | Screenshot or cropped observation, visible scope and action, accessible name, preserved focus | Keep the missing viewport or native observation open. |
| AC-002 | SCN-004 | Click once on a ready fixture in the installed side panel with no timing checkpoint. Observe actual publish, claim and completion. | Isolated Codex Desktop | Matched preflight, ready fixture and a human click | Desktop maintainer | One gesture, one send and one admitted claim with the original attempt | Preserve the original handoff when a claim cannot be observed. |
| AC-002 | SCN-005 | Assert the response and allowed follow-up on each winning route. Compare canonical state before and after the status read. | MCP integration suite and the single-click fixture | Bound disposable Run and readable before/after state | Core maintainer | Read-only response and marker, stable revision, zero gate effects | Block fixture use if a work plan or mutation can be granted. |
| AC-002 | SCN-006 | Match the receiving reply and lifecycle association to the count and claim from SCN-004. Include a missing-event control in the exporter test. | Desktop receiving chat and local evidence check | Case-local observation of receiving turn and hooks | Desktop maintainer | Receiving turn alias, case marker and matching claim, or a named missing fact | Leave the native click-to-turn result open if correlation fails. |
| AC-003 | SCN-007 | Open two ready cards. Publish from one, hold the receiving claim, then click the other. Release and inspect both cards. Also run the existing independent-process contention test. | Two native chats and Core suite | Same ready fixture on both sides and an observed contention window | Desktop maintainer | Exactly one Companion send and admitted turn, second view receiving pending or stale state | Repeat with a fresh fixture if overlap was missed; record missing overlap if it cannot be established. |
| AC-003 | SCN-008 | Use two fresh fixtures for the two orderings. First compete from chat against a pending Companion send. Then hold a chat winner's bounded reply and exercise a previously ready card. Inspect a second independent fixture. | Two native chats in the isolated profile | Ordinary governed chat route, existing Companion route, fixture checkpoint | Desktop maintainer | One work grant per race, losing route disposition, expected send counts and unaffected second fixture | Record precisely which ordering or peer route could not be observed. |
| AC-004 | SCN-009 | Pause between publish and claim. Close and reopen the panel, check status, then release. Finish normally. On a separate fixture interrupt the matched running turn. | Actual Desktop panel and chat | Observable pending handoff, continuation store and lifecycle hooks | Desktop maintainer | Same attempt and count on reopen, matched Stop and Interrupt on their respective cases | Preserve any uncertain attempt; never start another request to prove its outcome. |
| AC-004 | SCN-010 | Cancel before publication, withhold a post-publication response, send a conflicting terminal observation in a test fixture, and change a selected revision. Exercise the controls and poll. | Admission suite, component suite and built browser | Disposable fixtures with controlled events | Companion UI and Core | Ready-only explicit retry, pending remains protected, stale view reloads, zero sends on status checks | If ambiguity permits retry, block the case and reproduce in the admission suite. |
| AC-005 | SCN-011 | Test mismatched generations and roots at both entries. Withdraw activation with an open view and repeat the start attempt. | Runtime fixtures and separate isolated candidate sessions | Verified predecessor and successor identities and separate test roots | Codex integration | Per-route identity and refusal, old activation issuing no grant | Mark unverifiable identity as ineligible and keep the original evidence. |
| AC-005 | SCN-012 | In isolated upgrade trials, capture settled and outstanding predecessor states. Install the named successor, open a fresh session and check both routes before requesting work. Also test loss of continuity in a disposable copy. | Native isolated upgrade trials plus deterministic fixtures | Both candidate packages available, preserved per-trial admission store, no effects on delivery Runs | Desktop maintainer | One bounded fresh start on the settled path; zero new starts and known reason on pending, mixed or missing-continuity paths | Retain the predecessor evidence and report the missing trial or route. |
| AC-006 | SCN-013 | Compare matched and incomplete case records against the original readback. Provide a foreign candidate, uncorrelated turn and missing source as negative inputs. | Case exporter tests with disposable data | Case-local aliases and known expected observations | Codex integration | Explicit missing facts, candidate binding and no inferred native pass | Correct the case record before sharing it for qualification. |
| AC-006 | SCN-014 | Review the handoff against the linked plan. Reconstruct counts and chronology and check that private content has been removed. | Local native evidence review | Case records, available cropped observations, reviewer access to the fixture evidence | QA reviewer | Scenario index keyed to candidate and case with observations, conflicts and gaps | Return the record for redaction or name the missing observation for the operator. |

**Evidence handoff.** Cross-reference this Run's SCN-004 and SCN-006 to linked SCN-002, SCN-009 to linked SCN-009, SCN-007 and SCN-008 to both variants of linked SCN-011, and SCN-011 and SCN-012 to linked SCN-012. Give each trial a case alias and store only the candidate and host versions, fixture/attempt/turn aliases, ordered event sources, counts and disposition needed for review. Preserve consistent aliases within the trial. Exclude credentials, project paths outside the fixture, full conversations and raw unrelated hook events. The handoff records what was observed; its recipient makes the qualification decision.

## Planning Decisions

| Decision | Source | Status | Resolution | Owner |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Manual timing, coverage and redaction | SD: Timing and capture procedure | resolved | Use fresh case-scoped fixtures and one unpaused native baseline. At defined boundaries hold only the fixture's claim or reply, observe the peer before release and retain case-local aliases and separate counts. Cross-link both contention variants and the upgrade trials to the linked plan. | AGDF integration and QA |
| Test order and stopping condition | TP | resolved | Complete deterministic boundary, browser and packaging checks before manual exercise. Treat each missing native source as an explicit gap. Repeat only affected scenarios when the candidate, test conditions or evidence change. | QA reviewer |

## 4. Brownfield Scope

Inspect the current profile launcher, provenance owner, qualification and admission roots during Brownfield Analysis. Reuse `mcp-protocol-runtime.js` for candidate identity, `mcp-dispatch-runtime.js` and `cockpit-session.js` for the two entry points, `codex-continuation-binding.js` for canonical currency, and `codex-continuation.js` and `codex-activity.js` for reservation, claim and terminal correlation. Extend the existing Companion card, action and status read. Compare the observable fixture states to the brand source identified in SD and retain the evidence under the original acceptance IDs for TP review.

## 5. Out Of Scope

Production enablement, gate approvals, changes on other hosts, Desktop automation, arbitrary work in fixture turns, tests on delivery Runs and publication are outside this plan. A fixture timing control is available only in the isolated harness. Ordinary production qualification and governance remain with their existing owners.

## 6. Risks And Blockers

A second work grant, cross-profile effect, unbounded fixture response or automatic retry of a published unresolved attempt blocks readiness. Missing host access, unavailable timing control, unmatched candidate identities or incomplete event capture leave their native cases open. Keep the fixture and record of unresolved work for investigation. Deactivate further admission on teardown. Remove a fixture workspace only after its attempts have a known disposition and its requested evidence has been retained. Technical verification ends after the required observable results and targeted rechecks are recorded; mandatory reviews and human decisions still follow.

## 7. Next Step

The maintainer reviews the tasks, native cases and evidence boundaries. Approval of this plan permits the implementation-preparation Brownfield Analysis and subsequent implementation under canonical gate control.

## AGDF Approval Summary (de; source=en)

- Der Plan beginnt mit einem abgegrenzten Testprofil. Nur ein eigens angelegter Wegwerf-Run darf den Testauftrag ausführen. Er liest seinen Status und meldet die Fallkennung im Chat zurück.
- Zuerst prüfen automatisierte Tests die beiden Zugangswege, die Doppelstartsperre und die Anzeige im Begleiter. Danach klickt eine Person im isolierten Codex-Desktop-Profil auf „Run fortsetzen“. Der Nachweis verbindet diesen Klick mit genau einem empfangenden Codex-Turn.
- Zwei weitere Fälle prüfen die Konkurrenz zwischen zwei Begleitern sowie zwischen Begleiter und Chat. Nach erneutem Öffnen, bei Abbruch und nach einem Update muss die ursprüngliche Übergabe nachvollziehbar bleiben.
- Der verknüpfte Run erhält pro Fall die geprüfte Fassung, die beobachteten Starts und etwaige Lücken. Mit deiner Freigabe beginnt die Bestandsanalyse zur Vorbereitung der Umsetzung.
